EXT. ENCHANTED ORCHARD, STONE WELL - DAWN

Apple trees hang heavy with silver fruit. Mist curls over the grass. A moss-green well stands at the center, its rim carved with runes that faintly glow.

ALARIC THORNE, thirties, a wandering knight in a travel-stained cloak, kneels at the well's edge, lowering a leather bucket on a rope. His horse grazes nearby.

The bucket rises. He tips it and drinks from his hands. The water glitters.

A branch snaps behind him. He spins, hand on his hilt.

PERCIVAL ASHDOWN, thirties, in a scholar's gray robe, steps out of the trees with a satchel over one shoulder and a book in his hand. He raises his empty palm.

                    PERCIVAL
          Peace, friend. I mean no harm.

                    ALARIC
          Most who say that do.

                    PERCIVAL
          Then I shall say it quietly.

Alaric lowers his hand. Percival smiles, steps to the well, and peers over the rim.

                    PERCIVAL (CONT'D)
          You've drunk from it.

                    ALARIC
          I was thirsty.

                    PERCIVAL
          Of course. Everyone is.

He closes his book and tucks it into the satchel.

                    ALARIC
          What does it do?

                    PERCIVAL
          It shows you what you've lost.

                    ALARIC
          I've lost nothing.

                    PERCIVAL
          Then you won't mind looking.

He nods at the well. Alaric hesitates, then bends over the rim. The water stills. Something pale rises in it. He watches it for a long moment, and his shoulders go slack.

He turns away from the well and wipes his face with his sleeve. He does not look at the scholar, who has the decency to look at the trees.

                    ALARIC (CONT'D)
          How far is the nearest village?

                    PERCIVAL
          Half a day. I will walk with you, if you like.

                    ALARIC
          I prefer to walk alone.

                    PERCIVAL
          Then I shall walk very far behind.

Alaric almost smiles. He gathers his horse's reins and starts across the orchard. Percival waits until the knight is a dozen paces ahead, then follows, whistling softly.
